   "text": "I read the structure as a biotin\u2013PEG linker attached to a head-to-side-chain cyclic peptide. The ring is closed through a 1,4-disubstituted triazole.\n\n**Fragments, left to right**\n- **Biotin:** a bicyclic urea fused to a tetrahydrothiophene, with a valeryl chain, \u2013(CH2)4\u2013C(=O)\u2013.\n- **Linker:** NH\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH\u2013, a short PEG spacer ending in an amide to the first ring residue.\n- **Ring residues:** the peptide runs triazolyl-Ala \u2192 Leu \u2192 Leu \u2192 Gly \u2192 Trp \u2192 Pro \u2192 Lys-derived residue, which ends in a C-terminal primary amide (C(N)=O).\n- **Triazole bridge:** the Ala side chain is CH2 on triazole C4. Triazole N1 carries four CH2 groups running back to the Lys-derived \u03b1-carbon, which closes the macrocycle.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- I worked out the Ala(triazole) centre from its hashed bond to the CH2 and found it is L.\n- The other \u03b1-carbons (Leu, Leu, Trp, Pro, Lys-derived) are drawn with wedges and match the L configuration.\n- Gly has no stereocentre.\n- For the biotin ring fusion I used the standard natural biotin configuration. That is an assumption from recall rather than something I re-derived from the wedges.\n\n**Check:** I wrote the SMILES so that ring-closure digits are not reused while still open. The macrocycle closure (digit 8) sits on the Ala \u03b1-carbon and the Leu N-terminal C(=O). The chirality tags were assigned by permutation parity against the L-amino-acid pattern.\n\nSMILES: C1[C@H]2[C@@H]([C@@H](S1)CCCCC(=O)NCCOCCOCCOCC(=O)N[C@H]8Cc5cn(nn5)CCCC[C@@H](C(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@@H]6CCCN6C(=O)[C@H](Cc3c[nH]c4ccccc34)NC(=O)CNC(=O)[C@H](CC(C)C)NC(=O)[C@H](CC(C)C)NC8=O)NC(=O)N2"
  }
